$('.box').queue()               // Get queue
$('.box').dequeue()             // Process next
$('.box').clearQueue()          // Clear queue
$('.box').stop()                // Stop current animation
$('.box').stop(true)            // Stop and clear queue
$('.box').stop(true, true)      // Stop and jump to end
$('.box').finish()              // Jump to end of all animations

// Delay between animations
$('.box')
    .animate({ left: '100px' }, 300)
    .delay(500)                 // Wait 500ms
    .animate({ top: '100px' }, 300);
```

### Theory: CSS Transitions vs jQuery Animate

```javascript
// jQuery animate() (JavaScript-based)
$('.box').animate({ left: '100px' }, 300);
// Pros: Works on all browsers, callback, queue system
// Cons: Slower, blocking, no GPU acceleration

// CSS transitions (modern approach)
// .box { transition: left 0.3s ease; }
// Cons: Harder to detect completion, need to manage classes

// Modern approach (jQuery + CSS)
$('.box').addClass('animate');
// CSS: .box.animate { left: 100px; transition: left 0.3s; }
$(this).on('transitionend', function() {
    // Animation complete
});

// Best practice:
// ✓ Use CSS transitions for modern browsers
// ✓ Use jQuery animate as fallback
// ✓ Minimize JavaScript animations
```

